defined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3} \\\\ (n-3)(n+3)\\cdot \\frac{20}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =(n-3)(n+3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n-3}-\\frac{4}{n+3}) \\\\ \\frac{20(n-3)(n+3)}{(n-3)(n+3)} & =\\frac{3(n-3)(n+3)}{n-3}-\\frac{4(n-3)(n+3)}{n+3} \\\\ \\frac{20\\cdot \\cancel{(n-3)(n+3)}}{\\cancel{(n-3)(n+3)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{(n-3)}\\cdot (n+3)}{\\cancel{n-3}}-\\frac{4(n-3)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+3)}}{\\cancel{n+3}} \\\\ 20 & =3(n+3)-4(n-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =3(n+3)-4(n-3) \\\\ 20 & =3n+9-4n+12 \\\\ 20 & =-n+21 \\\\ n & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\ne -3$ and $1\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $n=1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=1.$" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263460 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263461 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263462 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263463 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263464 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1041 "selection" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263461 13194139533887 true] [17592186263459 1110 #uuid "602ab292-9e46-5e05-84b2-37106af2a5ad" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263460 1034 "No solutions" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533887 true] [17592186263460 1111 #uuid "b20d10e2-68cd-5b00-8d2c-2e882af681dc" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263461 1034 "n=1" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533887 true] [17592186263461 1111 #uuid "a6eaac11-9434-5142-8fbd-ff98ef7cfadc" 13194139533887 true] [17592186263462 1034 "n=-2" 13194139533887 tr